-- | Does a choice observer witness a non-consuming exercise, and does a plain
-- contract observer not?
--
-- PREDGE asked and answered this on forum.canton.network topic 9114, post 16,
-- on 2026-11-13, replying to tonozzibeverley953's pruning question of
-- 2026-11-11. His claim, quoted:
--
--   "Pruning is per participant: each node prunes only what it stores for the
--   parties it hosts. So the question becomes whether the auditor's own
--   participant ever receives the record. For the non-consuming path that
--   depends on who is an informee. A non-consuming exercise goes to
--   signatories, actors and choice observers, not to plain contract
--   observers. If the auditor is a choice observer on the refusal choice,
--   every refusal event lands on the auditor's participant, and the audited
--   party's pruning can't touch that copy."
--
-- WHY THIS MATTERS TO THIS PACKAGE. `Journal.daml` solves the same problem by
-- putting the auditor on the SIGNATORY line, which works because signatories
-- are informees of every exercise. That costs the auditor's authority at
-- creation time. If PREDGE is right, a choice observer gets the same delivery
-- for less: no authority needed, and the audited party still cannot prune the
-- auditor's copy, because pruning is per participant.
--
-- WHAT THIS SCRIPT CAN AND CANNOT ESTABLISH, stated before the result so the
-- result cannot be oversold. Daml Script has no primitive for reading an
-- event stream or asking who was an informee of an exercise node. It can only
-- ask what a party can see of the contract store. So this test does not
-- observe informee sets directly. It observes a CONSEQUENCE of them: the
-- non-consuming choice creates a contract on which neither the auditor nor
-- the bystander is a stakeholder, and asks which of them can then read it.
-- A witness to a create has it divulged; a party who never witnessed the
-- exercise does not.
--
-- So a pass here is evidence for PREDGE's claim and is not a proof of it.
-- The proof is a Ledger API transaction stream read per party, which needs
-- the sandbox and not Daml Script. That is named here rather than skipped.
module ChoiceObserverTest where

import Daml.Script

-- | Created by the non-consuming choice below. Neither the auditor nor the
-- bystander is a stakeholder, so anything either of them can see of it came
-- from witnessing the exercise and not from being on the contract.
template SignalTrace
  with
    owner : Party
    reason : Text
  where
    signatory owner

-- | `bystander` is a plain contract observer. `auditor` is named as an
-- observer ON THE CHOICE and nowhere else. That is the whole experiment.
template SignalSource
  with
    owner : Party
    auditor : Party
    bystander : Party
  where
    signatory owner
    observer bystander

    nonconsuming choice Signal : ContractId SignalTrace
      with
        reason : Text
      observer auditor
      controller owner
      do create SignalTrace with owner; reason

setup : Script (Party, Party, Party)
setup = do
  owner <- allocatePartyByHint (PartyIdHint "Owner")
  auditor <- allocatePartyByHint (PartyIdHint "Auditor")
  bystander <- allocatePartyByHint (PartyIdHint "Bystander")
  pure (owner, auditor, bystander)

-- | What Daml Script can establish here, which is a limitation and not a
-- result about Canton.
--
-- Run 13 November 2026 against SDK 3.4.11: NEITHER the choice observer NOR
-- the plain contract observer could read the contract the non-consuming
-- exercise created. So `queryContractId` does not expose a divulged contract
-- on this ledger, the proxy carries no signal either way, and this script
-- cannot answer PREDGE's question.
--
-- That is asserted rather than lamented, so it stays true: if a future SDK
-- starts exposing divulgence, this goes red and tells somebody the proxy has
-- become usable.
--
-- The question itself is answered by
-- `tools/who_witnesses_a_nonconsuming_exercise.py`, which reads the Ledger
-- API update stream once as each party. On this ledger the choice observer
-- receives the exercise and the plain contract observer does not, with a
-- signatory case measured alongside as a positive control.
testDamlScriptCannotSeeDivulgedContracts : Script ()
testDamlScriptCannotSeeDivulgedContracts = do
  (owner, auditor, bystander) <- setup

  src <- submit owner do
    createCmd SignalSource with owner; auditor; bystander

  -- The plain observer can see the source contract, which establishes that
  -- it really is on it, so anything it cannot see later is about the
  -- exercise and not about the party being a stranger.
  seenBySrc <- queryContractId bystander src
  case seenBySrc of
    None -> abort "the bystander cannot see the source contract it observes"
    Some _ -> pure ()

  trace <- submit owner do
    exerciseCmd src Signal with reason = "cap exceeded"

  byAuditor <- queryContractId auditor trace
  byBystander <- queryContractId bystander trace

  case (byAuditor, byBystander) of
    (None, None) -> pure ()
    _ -> abort "queryContractId now returns a divulged contract. The Daml \
               \Script proxy has become usable and this test should be \
               \rewritten to use it."

-- The signatory case is NOT scripted here, deliberately. It fails for the
-- same reason as the test above: `queryContractId` hides a divulged contract
-- whatever the party's informee status, so a Daml Script version would go red
-- and a reader would conclude that a signatory does not witness an exercise
-- on its own contract, which is false. The Ledger API says it plainly:
--
--     control: does a SIGNATORY witness a non-consuming exercise? yes
--
-- from `tools/who_witnesses_a_nonconsuming_exercise.py`, which uses the
-- template below through the JSON Ledger API. That is why `CoSignedSource`
-- exists in this module with no script of its own.

template CoSignedSource
  with
    owner : Party
    auditor : Party
    bystander : Party
  where
    signatory owner, auditor
    observer bystander

    nonconsuming choice CoSignedSignal : ContractId SignalTrace
      with
        reason : Text
      controller owner
      do create SignalTrace with owner; reason
